"Electronic Filing | First Appellate District | District Courts of Appeal (https://appellate.courts.ca.gov/district-courts/1dca/rules-forms-filing/electronic-filing)\nciteturn894search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Local Rule: Rule 12 (Local Rule page) | Local Rule: Rule 12 (PDF) ...   * Division Four (1DC-Div4-Clerks@jud.ca.gov) ... ## California Appellate Court Mandatory E-filing ... Notice: Notwithstanding the requirements set forth in California Rules of Court, Rule 8.500(f)(1), submission of a petition for review through TrueFiling that is accepted for filing by the Supreme Court constitutes service of the petition on the Court of Appeal.\n\n# E-Filing\n\nElectronic filing is the filing of an electronic document in lieu of a paper original and any required paper copies with the reviewing court.\n\nFile your documents electronically\n\nVisit TrueFiling\n\nSpecific E-filing Information for the First District\n\nLocal Rule: Rule 12 (Local Rule page) | Local Rule: Rule 12 (PDF)\n\nClerk’s Office contact: (415)865-7300\n\nDivisional clerks may be contacted by e-mail regarding pending cases as follows:\n\n  * Division One (1DC-Div1-Clerks@jud.ca.gov) \n  * Division Two (1DC-Div2-Clerks@jud.ca.gov) \n  * Division Three (1DC-Div3-Clerks@jud.ca.gov) \n  * Division Four (1DC-Div4-Clerks@jud.ca.gov) \n  * Division Five (1DC-Div5-Clerks@jud.ca.gov) \n\nUsing AI for Your Court Case?\n\nRead these guidelines first\n\nSpecial Instructions:\n\n  1. Paper copies do not need to be sent/delivered to the court if the document is submitted electronically.\n  2. Do not submit the document twice if you submit the incorrect document or notice a defect. Notify the deputy clerks in the assigned division requesting rejection of the submitted document and that a corrected version will be submitted.\n  3. For briefs and original proceedings, you must include a proof of service showing that the brief or writ petition was served by mail or personally delivered on the trial judge and/or superior court, unless the superior court explicitly permits electronic service.\n  4. If submitting document(s) on the due date, consider transmission time. Depending on the size of the document and/or bundle, transmission may take up to 30 minutes. (See Cal. Rules of Court, rule 8.77(c).)\n\n## California Appellate Court Mandatory E-filing\n\nPursuant to California Rules of Court, Rule 8.70 and Rule 8.71, all filings in Civil, Criminal, Juvenile and Original proceedings must be made through the Court's electronic filing system (TrueFiling).  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProposed New And Amended Local Court Rules And Forms | Superior Court of California | County of San Bernardino (https://sanbernardino.courts.ca.gov/news/proposed-new-and-amended-local-court-rules-and-forms-1)\nciteturn894search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; August 31, 2026 ... SAN BERNARDINO, CA— San Bernardino Superior Court (SBSC) proposes new and amended Local Rules of Court and Local Forms (attached) which will become effective on January 1, 2027.\n\nPublic Notice\n\n# Proposed New And Amended Local Court Rules And Forms\n\nAugust 31, 2026\n\nImage: Letterhead with court name, court seal, name, department and email address of public information office, and names of presiding judge and executive officer\n\n## Proposed New and Amended Local Court Rules and Forms\n\nPublic Notice\n\nRelease Date: September 1, 2026\n\nSAN BERNARDINO, CA— San Bernardino Superior Court (SBSC) proposes new and amended Local Rules of Court and Local Forms (attached) which will become effective on January 1, 2027.\n\nAll public comments must be made to SBSC’s Assistant General Counsel, Susan Jones, at courts-pio@sb-court.org. If you do not have internet access, comments may be mailed to: Superior Court of California, County of San Bernardino, Attention: Susan Jones, Assistant General Counsel, 247 West Third Street, San Bernardino, CA 92415-0210. All comments must be received no later than October 15, 2026. This notice has been posted on the court’s website at www.sb-court.org.\n\nFor media inquiries, contact Assistant General Counsel Susan Jones at courts-pio@sbcourt.org.\n\nAttachment--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNews & Announcements | First Appellate District | District Courts of Appeal (https://appellate.courts.ca.gov/district-courts/1dca/news-announcements)\nciteturn894search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Local Rules 13 and 15 are amended, and Local Rule 5 is repealed. ... The Court of Appeal, First Appellate District, is collaborating with the Justice & Diversity Center of the Bar Association of San Francisco (BASF) and the Appellate Law Section of BASF on a new, free civil self-help workshop.\n\n# News & Announcements\n\n## Search and Filter\n\n[Input: Search title or topic] Search News\n\n[Input]\n\n  * June 10, 2026\n\n## Impending Changes to Three Local Rules\n\nThe Court has approved changes to three of the First District’s Local Rules. Local Rules 13 and 15 are amended, and Local Rule 5 is repealed. The amended and repealed rules can be found here. These changes have been published in Advance Pamphlet 14, ...\n\n  * October 31, 2025\n\n## Using AI for Your Court Case? Read This First\n\nIf you're considering using AI tools like ChatGPT to help with your court case, please visit our \"Using AI?\" page to help you use these tools safely and effectively.\n\nGenerative AI is a tool that can help you write or explain things, but it can ma ...\n\n  * September 19, 2025\n\n## First District Court of Appeal Collaborates on Civil Self-Help Workshop\n\nThe Court of Appeal, First Appellate District, is collaborating with the Justice & Diversity Center of the Bar Association of San Francisco (BASF) and the Appellate Law Section of BASF on a new, free civil self-help workshop. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPractices & Procedures | First Appellate District | District Courts of Appeal (https://appellate.courts.ca.gov/district-courts/1dca/rules-forms-filing/practices-procedures)\nciteturn894search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Please Note: The information provided for civil appeals is in compliance with Local Rule 12. ... Code, § 21167.1) and general plans (Gov. ... Most docket sheets may be obtained online: See the Appellate Courts Case Information Search ... Yes, the Court of Appeal, First Appellate District now accepts debit cards and major credit cards—Visa, MasterCard, Discover, and American Express as a form of payment.\n\n# Practices & Procedures\n\nPlease Note: Self-represented parties are not required to use TrueFiling (electronic filing). If a self-represented party chooses to use electronic filing, they must follow Local Rule 12.\n\nUsing AI for Your Court Case? Read This First\n\n## \n\nNotice of Appeal & Record Preparation\n\n[Button: Expand all]\n\nFiling a notice of appeal\n\nCivil Appeal: A Notice of Appeal or cross appeal is filed with the clerk of the trial court from which the appeal is taken. California Rules of Court, Judicial Council (forms APP-001 and APP-002) may be used. A $775.00 filing fee or fee waiver is required.\n\nCriminal & Juvenile Appeals: A Notice of Appeal is filed with the clerk of the trial court from which the appeal is taken. Judicial Council (form CR-120 or JV-800 ) may be used. No filing fee is required. California Rules of Court, (rule 8.304 or 8.400)\nTime frames and service requirements for briefs\n\nPlease Note: The information provided for civil appeals is in compliance with Local Rule 12.\n\nFor Civil Appeals (CRC 8.212(a))\n\nDocument  | Filing Procedure  | Service Procedure\n--- | --- | ---\nAppellant's Opening Brief (AOB)  | \n\n  * 40 days from filing of record or\n  * 70 days from date of rule 8.124 election for cases without a Reporter's Transcript.\n | \n\n  * 4 paper copies on Supreme Court\n  * 1 copy on Superior Court\n  * 1 copy on each opposing party\nAppellant's Appendix  | \n\n  * No later than filing due date of AOB\n | \n\n  * 1 copy on each opposing party\nRespondent's Brief (RB)  | \n\n  * 30 days from filing of AOB\n | \n\n  * 4 paper copies on Supreme Court\n  * 1 copy on Superior Court\n  * 1 copy on each opposing party\nRespondent's Appendix  | \n\n  * 30 days from filing of AOB\n | \n\n  * 1 copy on each opposing party\nJoint Appendix  | \n\n  * No later than filing due date of AOB\n | \n\n  * None\nAppellant's Reply Brief (ARB)  | \n\n  * 20 days from filing of RB\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocal Rules: Rule 4 | Sixth Appellate District | District Courts of Appeal (https://appellate.courts.ca.gov/district-courts/6dca/rules-forms-filing/local-rules-orders/local-rules-rule-4)\nciteturn894search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Local Rule 4 – Establishing Appellate Jurisdiction, Civil Case Information Statement, Required Attachments ... Any additional documents that may be necessary to establish appellate jurisdiction pursuant to Code of Civil Procedure section 904.1 and California Rules of Court, rules 8.104 and 8.108.\n\n# Local Rules: Rule 4\n\n## \n\nRule 4 - Establishing Appellate Jurisdiction, Civil Case Information Statement, Required Attachments\n\nPublished pursuant to California Rules of Court, rule 10.1030 \n[Effective December 28, 2020]\n\nLocal Rule 4 – Establishing Appellate Jurisdiction, Civil Case Information Statement, Required Attachments\n\n  1. An appellant filing a completed Civil Case Information Statement (Form APP-004) in this District pursuant to California Rules of Court, rule 8.100 (g), must attach the following documents:\n\n \n\n    1. A copy of the judgment or order being appealed, including:\n      1. A judgment of dismissal, if the appeal challenges a judgment entered after:\n        1. an order granting summary judgment, or\n        2. an order sustaining a demurrer without leave to amend, or\n        3. an order granting judgment on the pleadings;\n      2. If appealing an order made after judgment (Code of Civ. Proc. §904.1, subd. (a)(2)), a copy of the final judgment or order of dismissal that predated the order being appealed (see Code Civ. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4dca3_guide-detailed (https://appellate.courts.ca.gov/sites/default/files/appellate/default/2023-09/4dca3_guide-detailed.pdf)\nciteturn894search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.4 years ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; (Local Rule 1(a), 1(b).) ... ** Where you receive a “Palma notice” (that the court is considering issuing a peremptory writ in the first instance), your opposition should fully and completely brief the legal and factual issues, since this may be your only opportunity to do so.\nD. Opposition to Writ Petition\n\n1. **Preliminary Opposition.** You are not required to oppose a writ petition, and you may elect to wait until invited to do so by the court. The court ordinarily will not issue an alternative writ, order to show cause or a peremptory writ unless you either have filed an opposition or unless the court has invited you to do so by written order. (Local Rule 1(a), 1(b).) However, the court may issue a stay or other order necessary to preserve the status quo or its jurisdiction without waiting for opposition. (Local Rule 1(a).)\n\n   a. **Filing.** Unless the court specifies a different due date, your preliminary opposition should be served and filed within 10 days after the petition is filed. (CRC 8.933(a).) Opposition to a petition for writ of supersedeas is due within 15 days, unless the court orders a different time. (CRC 8.112(b)(1).) There are no filing fees.\n\n   b. **Filing by Fax or E-mail.** Division Three does not accept filing of preliminary responses by fax or e-mail, unless the court has specifically requested that a copy of the response be faxed or emailed to the court by a specified time. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocal Rules of Court | Superior Court of California | County of Ventura (https://ventura.courts.ca.gov/general-information/local-rules-court)\nciteturn894search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;   * Effective July 1, 2026 Ventura County Local Rules of Court These rules are revised on January 1st and July 1st of each year. ... Proposed New and Revised Local Rules and Forms for January 1, 2027 ... Please email any questions or comments to: CommentsToProposedLocalRules@ventura.courts.ca.gov.\n\n# Local Rules of Court\n\n  * Effective July 1, 2026 Ventura County Local Rules of Court These rules are revised on January 1st and July 1st of each year.\n\n## Upcoming Revisions\n\nProposed New and Revised Local Rules and Forms for January 1, 2027\n\nRevisions to our local rules and forms are scheduled to take effect on January 1, 2027. All changes are shown in red. New text is underlined and deleted text is crossed out. \n\nProposed Changes to Local Rules - Effective January 1, 2027\n\nProposed Changes to Local Forms - Effective January 1, 2027\n\nThe Judges would appreciate any comments or recommendations regarding these rules on or before Monday, November 2, 2026. \n\nPlease email any questions or comments to: CommentsToProposedLocalRules@ventura.courts.ca.gov. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinal Local Rule 1 Amendment | District Courts of Appeal (https://appellate.courts.ca.gov/district-courts/3dca/publication/final-local-rule-1-amendment)\nciteturn894search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Final Local Rule 1 Amendment\n\nJune 26, 2023\n\n# Final Local Rule 1 Amendment\n\nPDF Amendment\n\nDownload PDF--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTitle (https://courts.ca.gov/system/files/rules-court/2025-10-24-rules-effective-01-01-2026_0.pd