/rules-forms-filing/local-rules-orders)\nciteturn894search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; > (1) “Court” means the Court of Appeal for the Third Appellate District. ... The court will reject an electronic filing if it does not comply with the requirements of the California Rules of Court and this local rule. ... Appellants shall be deemed to have challenged the decisions in Black II and Sandoval on any such federal constitutional grounds, and to have thereby preserved the issues for petition for review to the California Supreme Court and petitions for appellate or collateral review by the federal courts.\n(Adopted, eff. October 2, 2006. As amended, eff. March 2, 2007; As amended, eff. May 29, 2009; As amended, eff. December 2, 2013.; As amended, eff. May 26, 2017; As amended, eff. May 31, 2019; As amended eff. September 18, 2023.)\n\nRule 2: Stipulation for use of original superior court file\n\nRule 8.128 of the California Rules of Court provides for the use of the original superior court file in lieu of the clerk's transcript on appeal in those civil cases where the parties so stipulate. In accordance with rule 8.128 of the California Rules of Court, the procedure therein is approved for use by the superior courts within this district.\n\n(Formerly Rule 22, adopted, eff. September 4, 1989. Renumbered Rule 2, eff. October 2, 2006. As amended, eff. March 2, 2007.)\n\nRule 3: Time for oral argument\n\nEach side is allowed 15 minutes for oral argument. Where there are more than two parties, a \"side\" consists of all parties whose interests are not adverse. If there are more than two parties represented by separate counsel who request oral argument, or if counsel for amicus curiae requests oral argument, the court may apportion or expand the time according to the respective interests of the parties and of amicus curiae.\n(Adopted, eff. October 2, 2006.)\n\nRule 5: Electronic Filing\n\nPublished pursuant to California Rules of Court, rule 10.1030\n\nEffective September 14, 2015\n\nAmended effective September 26, 2016\n\nAmended effective December 11, 2017\n\nAmended effective April 10, 2020\n\nAmended effective February 24, 2025\n\nAmended effective April 10, 2026\n\n(a) Definitions\n\nAs used in this local rule, unless the context otherwise requires:\n\n> (1) “Court” means the Court of Appeal for the Third Appellate District.\n> \n> (2) “Electronic filing” and “document” are as defined in rule 8.70(c), California Rules of Court.\n> \n> (3) A “file” is a unit of electronic information with a filename.\n> \n> (4) “TrueFiling” is the court’s electronic filing portal for registered users.\n> \n> (5) “Registered user” and “registered users” refer to a person or persons registered to use TrueFiling.\n> \n> (6) “EFS” means the court’s electronic filing system, which includes, but is not limited to, TrueFiling and the court’s file transfer protocol (FTP) server.\n(l) Rejection of an Electronic Filing for Noncompliance\n\nThe court will reject an electronic filing if it does not comply with the requirements of the California Rules of Court and this local rule.\n\n(m) Sanctions for Noncompliance\n\nFailure of counsel to timely register, and failure of any registered user to comply with electronic filing requirements, unless exempted, may be subject to sanctions imposed by the court.\n\n(n) Original Documents\n\nThe court may, in its discretion, scan any paper document into an electronic format, in which case the electronic document will be deemed the original for purposes of the court record.\n\n(o) Posting and Publication\n\nThe Clerk of the Court is directed to post a copy of this local rule on the court’s Web site and submit a copy to the Reporter of Decisions for publication.\n\nDated: January 28, 2026\n\nMiscellaneous Orders\n\n[Button: Expand all]\n\nMISC. ORDER 2026-001\n\nMiscellaneous Orders 2009-008 and 2007-003 are hereby vacated.\n\nDated: January 15, 2026.\n\nEARL, P.J.\n\nMISC. ORDER 2013-002\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocal Rules & Orders | Fifth Appellate District | District Courts of Appeal (https://appellate.courts.ca.gov/district-courts/5dca/rules-forms-filing/local-rules-orders)\nciteturn894search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; > (1) Effective upon the filing of any civil notice of appeal to which the rule applies, the provisions of rules 8.121, 8.124 and 8.216 of the California Rules of Court requiring designation of the record, payment of estimated costs for preparation of the record, and submission of a proposed briefing schedule are suspended, pending the Court’s decision to select or not select the civil appeal for mediation. ... Any date selected for a motion for sanctions must be cleared in advance with the Court’s Mediation Coordinator at 5DC_Mediation@jud.ca.gov. ... The court of Appeal, Fifth Appellate District, repeals its Local Rule of Court, Rule 5.\n\n# Local Rules & Orders\n\n## \n\nLocal Rules Unique to the Fifth Appellate District\n\nCounsel and self-represented litigants should be familiar with the California Rules of Court, especially the Appellate Rules. (See Cal. Rules of Court, rule 8.1 et seq.) The local rules below are intended to highlight the practices unique to the Fifth Appellate District and to emphasize portions of the Rules of Court that the court considers to be most important.\n\nRule 1. Augmentation of Record and Correction of Omissions from Record\n\n(a) [Material inadvertently omitted] Counsel should not file a motion to augment the record when items have been omitted from the normal (criminal) record on appeal. In those cases counsel should immediately notify the clerk of the superior court, who shall comply with rule 8.155 (b) of the California Rules of Court.\n\n(b) [When to file motion to augment] Appellant should file requests for augmentation in one motion within 40 days of the filing of the record. (f) [Good faith required] A motion to augment shall be made in good faith and shall not be made for the purpose of delay.\n\n(Formerly Rule 2, adopted, eff. Nov. 15, 1993. Renumbered Rule 1, eff. Aug. 25, 2003. As amended, eff. March 2, 2007; Aug.17, 2007; Feb. 19, 2016.)\n\nRule 2. Mediation Program for Civil Appeals\n\nThe Court has established a Judicial Mediation Program to assist parties to resolve appellate disputes in civil cases the Court finds suitable.\n\n(a) Rule application: This rule applies to all civil appeals except those under Welfare and Institutions Code sections 300, 601, and 602 and Family Code section 7802, appeals in conservatorship and guardianship matters, and appeals from original proceedings ancillary to a criminal prosecution.\n\n(b) Questionnaire: When a notice of appeal is filed in any civil case to which this rule applies, the Clerk will promptly mail or e-mail to appellant and respondent instructions for the filing of the nonconfidential Civil Appeal Case Screening Questionnaire. (Downloadable form available on the Court’s website) Within 10 calendar days after the Clerk mails or e-mails instructions regarding the Civil Appeal Case Screening Questionnaire, each party must complete, sign, and serve the Questionnaire, and file it electronically.\n\nAppellate Court Writ Petition Information Sheet\n\nBy order dated November 23, 1982, all petitions for extraordinary writs, other than habeas corpus, shall be accompanied by a properly completed face sheet, the Appellate Court Writ Petition Information Sheet. This form is available from the clerk of the court, and also is available on the court website.\n\n(As amended, eff. Aug. 25, 2003.)\n\nOrders\n\nMisc. Order Vacating Misc. Order 2017-1 Re Reporter's Transcripts for Appointed Counsel - August 1, 2025\n\nAdministrative Order – March 10, 2023\n\nAdministrative Order – March 14, 2022\n\nEmergency Order – April 20, 2020\n\nEmergency Order – April 16, 2020\n\nJudicial Council Emergency Order – April 15, 2020\n\nEmergency Order – April 13, 2020\n\nEmergency Order – March 23, 2020\n\nJudicial Council Emergency Order – March 23, 2020\n\nEmergency Order – March 17, 2020\n\nOrder Regarding Revisions to Local Rule 2 - Adopts Mediation Program in Civil Appeals - June 14, 2011\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRules Committee Meeting Binder (September 9 and 10, 2026) (https://courts.ca.gov/system/files/file/rules-20260910-materials.pdf)\nciteturn894search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 weeks ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; courts.ca.gov ... For business meeting on October 16, 2026 ... The Appellate Advisory Committee recommends that the Judicial Council, effective January 1, 2027, approve *Order on Application for Extension of Time to File Brief—Limited Civil, Misdemeanor, or Infraction Case* (form APP-106(O)/CR-127(O)), for optional use.\nJudicial Council of California  \n455 Golden Gate Avenue · San Francisco, California 94102-3688  \ncourts.ca.gov\n\nREPORT TO THE JUDICIAL COUNCIL\n\nItem No.: 26-129  \nFor business meeting on October 16, 2026\n\nAppellate Procedure: Extension-of-Time Order in Limited Civil, Misdemeanor, and Infraction Appeals (DRAFT)\n\n## Judicial Council Report Details\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Rules, Forms, Standards, or Statutes Affected | Approve form APP-106(O)/CR-127(O) |\n| Effective Date | January 1, 2027 |\n| Recommended by | Appellate Advisory Committee<br>Hon. Allison M. Danner, Chair |\n| Date of Report | August 18, 2026 |\n| Report Type | Action Required |\n| Contact | Nikki Marquez, 415-865-7990<br>nikki.marquez@jud.ca.gov |\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe Appellate Advisory Committee recommends approving a new optional form for issuing an order on an application for an extension of time to file a brief in limited civil, misdemeanor, and infraction appeals. This proposal originated from a suggestion by the Committee on Appellate Courts of the California Lawyers Association’s Litigation Section.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nThe Appellate Advisory Committee recommends that the Judicial Council, effective January 1, 2027, approve *Order on Application for Extension of Time to File Brief—Limited Civil, Misdemeanor, or Infraction Case* (form APP-106(O)/CR-127(O)), for optional use.\n\nThe proposed new form is attached at page 5.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRule 5.324. Telephone appearance in title IV-D hearings and conferences | Judicial Branch of California (https://courts.ca.gov/cms/rules/index/five/rule5_324)\nciteturn894search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; California Rules of Court 2026 ... Rule 5.324 amended effective January 1, 2026; adopted effective July 1, 2005; previously amended effective January 1, 2007, January 1, 2008, July 1, 2008, July 1, 2011, January 1, 2014, January 1, 2017, January 1, 2022, and August 4, 2023.\n\nCalifornia Rules of Court 2026\n\n# Rule 5.324. Telephone appearance in title IV-D hearings and conferences\n\n(a) Purpose\n\nThis rule is suspended from January 1, 2022, to January 1, 2027. During that time, the provisions in rule 3.672 apply in its place.\n\n(Subd (a) amended effective January 1, 2026; previously amended effective January 1, 2022, and August 4, 2023.)\n\n(b) Definition\n\n\"Telephone appearance,\" as used in this rule, includes any appearance by telephonic, audiovisual, videoconferencing, digital, or other electronic means.\n\n(c) Permissibility of telephone appearances\n\nUpon request, the court, in its discretion, may permit a telephone appearance in any hearing or conference related to an action for child support when the local child support agency is providing services under title IV-D of the Social Security Act.\n\n(d) Exceptions\n\nA telephone appearance is not permitted for any of the following except as permitted by Family Code section 5700.316:\n\n(1) Contested trials, contempt hearings, orders of examination, and any matters in which the party or witness has been subpoenaed to appear in person; and\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRule 3.670. Telephone appearance | Judicial Branch of California (https://courts.ca.gov/cms/rules/index/three/rule3_670)\nciteturn894search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; California Rules of Court 2026 ... (Subd (b) amended effective January 1, 2026; previously repealed and adopted as subd (a) effective July 1, 1998; previously relettered effective January 1, 2008; previously amended effective January 1, 1999, January 1, 2001, January 1, 2003, January 1, 2007, January 1, 2022, and August 4, 2023.)\n\nCalifornia Rules of Court 2026\n\n# Rule 3.670. Telephone appearance\n\n(a) Policy favoring telephone appearances\n\nThe intent of this rule is to promote uniformity in the practices and procedures relating to telephone appearances in civil cases. To improve access to the courts and reduce litigation costs, courts should permit parties, to the extent feasible, to appear by telephone at appropriate conferences, hearings, and proceedings in civil cases.\n\n(Subd (a) adopted effective January 1, 2008.)\n\n(b) Application\n\nSubdivisions (c) through (i) of this rule are suspended from January 1, 2022, to January 1, 2027, during which time the provisions in rule 3.672 apply in their place. This rule applies to all general civil cases as defined in rule 1.6 and to unlawful detainer and probate proceedings.\n\n(Subd (b) amended effective January 1, 2026; previously repealed and adopted as subd (a) effective July 1, 1998; previously relettered effective January 1, 2008; previously amended effective January 1, 1999, January 1, 2001, January 1, 2003, January 1, 2007, January 1, 2022, and August 4, 2023.)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(Revised) E-Binder: Rules Committee Meeting_January_7_2026 (https://courts.ca.gov/system/files/file/revised-rules-20260107-materials.pdf)\nciteturn894search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; effective January 1, 2027, all of which would be part of the Spring 2026 invitation-to-comment cycle. ... - The committee will develop a recommendation to amend rule 5.275 to add provisions regarding the certification and recertification processes, including (1) revocation of a calculator’s certification and (2) allowing for an exception to the requirement that the California Child Support Guideline Calculator be used in title IV-D proceedings when its certification has been revoked or the calculator is deemed inaccessible for court use.The committee will also consider whether rule 5.275 should be amended to address related provisions regarding recertification of a support calculator, such as when a software developer should be granted an extension of time to provide a calculator for certification or when California’s Child Support Guideline Calculator could be deemed inaccessible in other limited circumstances.\nRules Committee  \nJanuary 2, 2026  \nPage 3\n\neffective January 1, 2027, all of which would be part of the Spring 2026 invitation-to-comment cycle.\n\nThe specifications for the item would be:\n\n- Project title: Family Law: Amend rule 5.275 to update the calculator certification and recertification process and to add limited exceptions for mandated calculator use.\n- The committee will develop a recommendation to amend rule 5.275 to add provisions regarding the certification and recertification processes, including (1) revocation of a calculator’s certification and (2) allowing for an exception to the requirement that the California Child Support Guideline Calculator be used in title IV-D proceedings when its certification has been revoked or the calculator is deemed inaccessible for court use. The committee will also consider whether rule 5.275 should be amended to address related provisions regarding recertification of a support calculator, such as when a software developer should be granted an extension of time to provide a calculator for certification or when California’s Child Support Guideline Calculator could be deemed inaccessible in other limited circumstances.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocal Rules of Court | Placer County Superior Court (https://placer.courts.ca.gov/index.php/forms-filing/local-rules-court)\nciteturn894search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   * Local Rules of Court, effective January 1, 2026  * Local Rules of Court_Effective January 1 2027_DRAFT.pdf & Attachment 1_Local Court Forms_January 1 2027.pdf - Open for mandatory comment period. ... Comments must be submitted in writing via email to courtadmin@placer.courts.ca.gov or by mail to the attention of Court Administration.\n\n# Local Rules of Court\n\n## Local Rules\n\n  * Local Rules of Court, effective January 1, 2026\n\n  * Local Rules of Court_Effective January 1 2027_DRAFT.pdf & Attachment 1_Local Court Forms_January 1 2027.pdf - Open for mandatory comment period. The Court will accept comments on the proposed amendments through November 2, 2026. Comments must be submitted in writing via email to courtadmin@placer.courts.ca.gov or by mail to the attention of Court Administration.\n\n## California Rules of Court\n\nThe California Rules of Court are located on the California Courts Website.\n\n> The forms that are available on this website are in PDF format and require a PDF reader to view them. Adobe Reader, is available for free and allows you to view, navigate, and print these PDF files. To get Adobe Reader, visit the Adobe website.\n\n#### Locations\n\nYou can purchase a paperback copy of the local rules from one of the following locations:\n\nHon. Howard G. Gibson Courthouse\n10820 Justice Center Drive\nRoseville, CA 95678\n916-408-6000\n8:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.\n\nTahoe Courthouse\n2501 North Lake Blvd.\nTahoe City, CA 96145\n530-584-3460\n8:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m."
